% Solutions to the Chapter 5 exercises.
accumulate pond.

% ANCHOR: ex1
% Exercise 5.1: `swims heron 2` is turned away: swims
% takes one critter, and here it is given two
% arguments. The other two lines are well typed. Both
% are false: no fact says the otter flies, and nothing
% in the pond eats itself.
pred ex1.
ex1 :-
  (flies otter, print "flies otter: yes"
  ; print "flies otter: no"),
  (eats duck duck, print "eats duck duck: yes"
  ; print "eats duck duck: no").
% ANCHOR_END: ex1

% ANCHOR: ex2
% Exercise 5.2: a new kind with two constants.
% `size-of frog 3` never runs: the checker reads the
% whole file first and stops at the 3, an int where a
% size belongs. No question is asked at all.
kind size type.
type small, big size.

pred size-of o:critter, o:size.
size-of heron big.
size-of otter big.
size-of duck big.
size-of frog small.
size-of fish small.

pred ex2.
ex2 :- std.findall (size-of X big) L, print L.
% ANCHOR_END: ex2

% ANCHOR: ex3
% Exercise 5.3: A and B are two type variables, so the
% two lists may hold different types. Declared with
% `list A, list A`, the call below is turned away:
% the critters fix A, and 1 and 2 are ints.
pred same-len o:list A, o:list B.
same-len [] [].
same-len [_ | T] [_ | U] :- same-len T U.

pred ex3.
ex3 :-
  same-len [frog, fish] [1, 2],
  print "same length".
% ANCHOR_END: ex3

% ANCHOR: ex4
% Exercise 5.4: `eater X frog` succeeds with X = heron.
% The i: is checked only where the question meets the
% head. The head `eater X Y` has a fresh variable in
% the first place, which matches the hole without
% filling it. Then the body asks `eats X frog`, and eats
% has o: modes, so unification fills X. `hunter` has
% the constant heron in its head, and matching cannot
% fill the caller's hole with it: no answers.
pred eater i:critter, o:critter.
eater X Y :- eats X Y.

pred hunter i:critter, o:critter.
hunter heron frog.
hunter heron fish.

pred ex4.
ex4 :-
  std.findall (eater X frog) E, print E,
  std.findall (hunter Y frog) H, print H.
% ANCHOR_END: ex4

main :- ex1, ex2, ex3, ex4.
